  "reproduction_readme": "Windows Python 3.14.3, pure Python project with cryptography/PyNaCl and DNS-library dependencies; no container or external runtime service. All validation uses generated RSA keys and an in-memory DNS callback. Build a fresh venv, install verification/acceptance-r2/runtime-requirements.txt, then install the exact product source with pip install --no-deps -e . . Do not copy the old editable baseline URL from raw pip freeze. Put the product root and verification/acceptance-r2 on PYTHONPATH; confirm dkim.__file__ points to the frozen source.\n\nRun python -B -m pytest -q -o addopts= -p no:cacheprovider --tb=short verification/acceptance-r2/test_dkim_contract.py verification/acceptance-r2/test_dkim_exact_contract.py. Frozen result: 16 pass, 3 fail. The three failures have two causes. A genuine valid signature with declared l=999 and a shorter complete canonical body is accepted even though the request requires exact l equality. This is an explicit display-policy mismatch, not a claim that these particular inputs contain unsigned appended bytes. Secondly a custom required-header list omitting From causes false rejection of an ordinary signed mail which actually contains exactly one From; _check_display_policy only counts From when it belongs to the caller list. Two list variants exercise the same root cause.\n\npython -B verification/acceptance-r2/test_dkim_exact_contract.py prints the cryptographic positive control and actual false strict acceptance. mail-check.py prints normal mail, rejected unsigned tails, independent weak/strong signatures, the false rejection and the oversized-l case. The first acceptance revision retained a faulty re-signing helper that omitted a CRLF after replacing b=; its ordinary verification assertion correctly failed before display-policy testing. Revision 2 adds the missing separator, does not edit product source, and uses a passing cryptographic control. That helper error is excluded from the score.\n\nThe model feature module has 22 pytest collected passes: 21 unittest test methods plus the test_suite helper, which yields a PytestReturnNotNoneWarning. Whole dkim/tests has 130 collected passes and 9 such warnings; feature results overlap the package suite and are not additive. The native python test.py reported 121 and a separate ARC suite of 2, so the final-answer wording calling 121 old tests plus 21 new tests should not be read as a sum of unique tests. Exact command output and scopes remain in the evidence. Recorded commands ran from this exact replay, continuously for 75 seconds with success and failures visible; the console runner is a separately authored verification helper.",
